<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.3.2: Plotting and Analyzing Residuals</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00735</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.3.2: Plotting and Analyzing Residuals</md:title>
    <md:uuid>769e6e1e-82c6-45a4-b113-abab8c151fb1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Video still of oranges being weighed">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_03_01.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <!--BEGIN ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <!--START QUESTION # 1-->
      <para>Here is the data table for orange weights from a previous lesson. Use this information to answer the questions.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis> number of oranges</emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis> weight in kilograms</emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>3 </entry>
            <entry>1.027 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>4 </entry>
            <entry>1.162 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>5 </entry>
            <entry>1.502 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>6 </entry>
            <entry>1.617 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>7 </entry>
            <entry>1.761 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>8 </entry>
            <entry>2.115 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>9 </entry>
            <entry>2.233 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>10 </entry>
            <entry>2.569 </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Create the scatter plot and calculate the best fit line using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--END ADD DESMOS INTERATIVE-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            
            <para>After you have finished graphing, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>The equation of the best fit line is \(y=0.216x+0.345\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>2. What level of accuracy makes sense for the slope and intercept values? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Rounding the values to the thousandths place makes sense because that would be to an accuracy of 1 gram, which is what the original data used.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>3. What does the linear model estimate for how much the weight increases for of each additional box of oranges?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>0.216 kg for each orange</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>4. Compare the weight of the actual box with 3 oranges in it to the estimated weight of the box with 3 oranges in it. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The actual weight is 1.027 kg, and the estimated weight is 0.993 kg. Therefore, the actual weight is 0.034 kg greater than the estimate.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>5. How many oranges are in the box when the linear model estimates the weight best? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>6 oranges are in the box when the estimate is best. The difference between the estimated weight and the actual weight is only 0.023 kg, while the other points are farther from the line.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>6. How many oranges are in the box when the linear model estimates the weight least well? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>7 oranges are in the box when the estimate is worst. The difference between the estimated weight and the actual weight is 0.095, which is the most of any of the values.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <para>Use the following information to answer questions 7 and 8.</para>
      <para>
        <quote>The difference between the actual value and the value estimated by a linear model is called the residual.</quote>
     </para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>7. For the orange weight data set, what is the residual for the line of best fit when there are 3 oranges?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The residual is \(0.034 (1.027−0.993=0.034)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>8. On the same axes as the scatter plot, plot this residual at the point where \(x=3\) and \(y\) has the value of the residual.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>The point is (8,0.043).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>9. Find the residuals for each of the other points in the scatter plot and graph them.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Blank coordinate plane with grid. Horizontal axis from 0 to 11 by 1's. Vertical axis from negative 0 point 5 to 0 point 5, by 0 point 1's.  ">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_03_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Discrete graph.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_03_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>10. Which point on the scatter plot has the residual closest to zero? What does this mean about the weight of the box with that many oranges in it?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>6 oranges. It means the estimated weight of the box with 6 oranges in it is closest to the actual value.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <newline/>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>11. How can you use the residuals to decide how well a line fits the data?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para>A line will result in more accurate estimates when the residuals are closer to zero.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Finding Residuals</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about finding residuals.</para>
      <newline/>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/23c4c5a71e02040cd50b142cc3fd9585b214bcaf"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check"><para>The equation for a line of best fit is \(y = 2.2x +3.4\). Find the residual for the point \((3, 9)\).</para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>Finding Residuals</title>
      <para>The gestation time for an animal is the typical duration between conception and birth. The longevity of an animal is the typical lifespan for that animal. The gestation times, in days, and longevities, in years, for 13 types of animals are shown in the table below.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Animal</emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Gestation Time
        (Days)</emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Longevity
        (Years)</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Baboon</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>187</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>20</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Black Bear</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>219</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>18</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Beaver</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>105</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>5</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Bison</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>285</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>15</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Cat</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>63</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>12</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Chimpanzee</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>23</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>20</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Cow</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>284</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>15</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Dog</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>61</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>12</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Fox (Red)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>52</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>7</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Goat</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>151</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>8</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Lion</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>100</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>15</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Sheep</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>154</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>12</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>Wolf</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>63</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>5</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para>The equation of a line of best fit is \(y = 9.875 + 0.02039x\), where \(x\) represents the gestational time, in days, and \(y\) represents longevity, in years.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_05_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>A lion’s gestation time is 100 days, and its longevity is 15 years. What does this line of best fit predict the lion’s longevity to be?</para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>The line of best fit is \(𝑦=9.875 + 0.02039(100)=11.9\)</para>
      <para>The residual would be: actual value – estimated value or \(15−11.9=3.1\).</para>
      <para>The residual value is 3.1.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Finding Residuals</title>
      <para>In the table above, a dog has a gestation time of 61 days and a longevity of 12 years.</para>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Using the same equation, \(y = 9.875 + 0.02039x\), what would the residual value be?</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Substitute \(x =61\) into the equation, \(𝑦=9.875 + 0.02039(61)=11.1\).</para>
            <para>So, the residual is the (actual value – estimated value):</para>
            <para>\(12−11.1=0.9\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
    </section>
    
